 "rulebook": "IFAB Laws of the Game 2025/26: Law 1 (the goal 7.32 m x 2.44 m with posts and crossbar 12 cm wide, the penalty mark 11 m from the goal line, the penalty area, the goal area), Law 2 (a size-5 ball: 68-70 cm round, 410-450 g), Law 10 (a goal: the whole ball over the goal line between the posts and under the bar), Law 12 (handball), Law 13 (9.15 m from the ball until it is in play) and the glossary (a kick is a contact with the foot or the ankle)",
 "deviations": [
  "The cross is a scripted launch: a fixed lofted kick with backspin from a point 20.0 m away on the left wing, the same in every attempt, instead of a team-mate.",
  "The goalkeeper is a dummy, a rigid board 1.9 m high and 0.6 m wide that stands still on the goal line at the centre of the goal: an opponent cannot be simulated.",
  "A volley is judged by numbers instead of a referee's eye: every contact within 0.15 s of the first is the one strike, and a strike is a ball that leaves the foot at least 1.0 times as fast as it arrived (a ball that only rebounds from a leg held in its path leaves slower: it is not a shot).",
  "Law 13's distance is applied to the robot itself: until the cross is played it stays 9.15 m from the ball, so that the cross is played, not met at the kicker.",
  "The Laws allow any number of touches and any part but the arms; the task is narrower than the Laws: one touch, with a foot or an ankle only, before the cross touches the ground (the task is a volley, not open play).",
  "Law 1: only the goal end of the field is there; the goal's net is a rigid cage 2.0 m deep; no wind, no other player."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Volley the cross: strike the football that is crossed to you from the wing with a foot before it touches the ground, into the official goal 11 m away, past the goalkeeper dummy.",
  "rules": "You stand on the penalty mark, facing the goal; the goal line is 11.0 m ahead of you. The goal is 7.32 m wide between the posts and 2.44 m high under the crossbar (posts and bar 0.12 m thick), centred on the line you face along. A goalkeeper dummy, a board 1.9 m high, 0.6 m wide and 0.3 m thick, stands still on the goal line at the centre of the goal. A size-5 football (0.22 m across, 0.43 kg, in air) waits on the ground on the left wing, 20.0 m from you, and 3.0 s after the start it is crossed: a lofted ball with backspin that comes down in front of you. The volley is the ball's first contact after the cross: with a foot or an ankle of yours (the parts below the shin), before the ball has touched the ground. Every contact within 0.15 s of the first belongs to the one strike, which is over when the ball has been clear of you for 0.03 s; and it must be a strike: the ball's speed when it leaves your foot is at least 1.0 times its speed just before the foot touched it. You succeed at the moment the whole ball is then over the goal line between the posts and under the bar; after the strike it may touch the ground, the posts, the bar and the dummy. You fail if the ball touches the ground before your strike, if any part of you other than a foot or an ankle touches the ball at any time, if the strike's contacts last longer than 0.15 s, if the ball leaves the foot slower than that, if you touch the ball again after the strike, if the whole ball crosses the goal line outside the goal, if after the strike the ball has been slower than 0.1 m/s for 1.0 s, if your pelvis comes within 9.15 m of the waiting ball before it is crossed, if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the ground, or any part of you other than the feet on the ground), or after 10 s. The waiting ball cannot be touched.",
  "venue": "You face the goal (+x) from the penalty mark. The ball waits on the ground about 6 m ahead of you and 19 m to your left. About 3 s after the start it is crossed; it climbs to a little over 4 m and comes down steeply (about 40 degrees below the horizontal) at roughly 11.5 m/s, travelling mostly from your left to your right. About 2 s after the cross it is knee-high about 0.6 m in front of you, a little to your left; untouched it lands about half a metre in front of you, on the side of your right foot.",
  "difficulty": "The ball crosses the reach of a leg in a few hundredths of a second, at 11 m/s and at right angles to the goal: the foot has to meet it in the air with a swing of its own, fast enough that the ball leaves faster than it came, aimed 11 m into the goal and beside the dummy, by a 67 kg robot on one leg whose walking policy has no kick."
 },
